  "summary": "Under-construction GIFs, MIDI autoplay and guestbooks — the web when it was rooms.",
  "description": "The GeoCities era (1994-2009) organized the amateur web as neighborhoods: personal homepages with tiled star backgrounds, animated GIF dividers, visitor counters, guestbooks, MIDI autoplays, \"under construction\" workers and webring navigation. Design was maximal, hand-coded and sincere — every page a bedroom; every hit counter, a heartbeat. Yahoo's 2009 shutdown triggered the Archive Team's emergency rip (now preserved by the GeoCities torrent and one terabyte of rooms), and the era's look now anchors \"old web\" nostalgia, neocities.org revivals, and yesterweb sentiment: the web as a place you live rather than a product you use.",
  "culturalContext": "The GeoCities era is the web's founding nostalgia: Archive Team's rescue rip preserved a million homepages, Neocities rebuilds the culture, and \"old web\" sentiment (webrings, guestbooks, under-construction GIFs) now anchors digital-preservation movements and critiques of platformized internet.",
